---
/**
 * What each store keeps about a visit, in plain terms, and what it drops. Every sentence
 * here has to stay true of api/src/worker/usage.ts, api/src/worker/demand.ts,
 * api/src/worker/index.ts, the beacon in site/src/components/Beacon.astro and
 * site/src/components/Search.astro, and workers/rollup/: those are the only places that
 * write a row, and the mirror of this page under /fr/ says the same in French.
 */
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import { REPO_URL } from "../../i18n/ui";

const locale = "en";

const toc = [
  { id: "requests", label: "Every request" },
  { id: "asked-for", label: "What’s asked for" },
  { id: "search", label: "A search" },
  { id: "kept", label: "What’s kept" },
  { id: "code", label: "The code" },
];

const file = (path: string) => `${REPO_URL}/blob/main/${path}`;
---

<Docs
  locale={locale}
  route="docs/privacy/"
  title="What this site counts"
  description="What this site keeps when someone searches, opens a place or calls a tool."
  lede="Two stores count how this site and its API get used. Neither keeps anything that ties a row to a visitor."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="requests">
    <h2>Every request</h2>
    <p>
      Every API call and MCP request is counted once, in Cloudflare’s Workers Analytics Engine. The row holds the
      route with its codes taken out, or an MCP message’s tool when it called one and its method otherwise, the
      name an MCP client gives itself, the first word of the User-Agent, the country, the status and how long the
      request took. That’s the whole row: enough to see how the API gets used, not who used it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A tool or a method outside a fixed list is stored as “other”, and so is a client name the scrub empties.
      Cloudflare keeps these rows for 3 months. A static file runs no code, so opening one isn’t counted here.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="asked-for">
    <h2>What’s asked for</h2>
    <p>
      A second store, in D1, counts 5 kinds of row: a search, a place opened, a tool an assistant calls through
      the MCP server, the name an MCP client gives itself, and a file downloaded. A row holds the day, what was
      asked for, the country, whether the User-Agent looks like a crawler’s, and which class of site sent the
      visitor, such as reddit or a search engine, never the address itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      The client that made the request is recorded too: “browser” for the site’s own search box and beacon, and
      the first word of the User-Agent for anyone using the API or MCP directly.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="search">
    <h2>A search</h2>
    <p>
      A search is stored lower-cased and cut to 64 characters. It’s dropped completely if it holds an @, a web
      address, more than 6 words, or 6 digits or more however they’re written, unless those digits spell out a
      real place’s code. That’s how an email or a phone number typed into the box by mistake stays out.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site’s own search box reports a search once it’s settled: 1.5 seconds after the results stop changing,
      or as soon as one is opened, never on every keystroke. Nothing is sent by the search box, or by the beacon
      that notes a place or a file opened, when the browser’s Do Not Track is on.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="kept">
    <h2>What’s kept</h2>
    <p>
      A row is a day and a handful of categories, the kind a tally needs and nothing more: no room in it for an
      address, a cookie, a session or any other identifier. 2 rows can never be tied to the same visitor.
    </p>
    <p>
      The demand log’s raw rows are deleted after 90 days. What’s left is counts by day, which the most
      looked-up page is built from. A search keeps its words in that count only when they name a place or were
      typed 3 or more times that day; otherwise only the count stays. The log can’t tell whether that’s one
      visitor typing it 3 times or 3 different visitors, since nothing in a row would tell them apart.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="code">
    <h2>The code</h2>
    <p>
      This runs from <a href={file("api/src/worker/usage.ts")}><code>api/src/worker/usage.ts</code></a>,{" "}
      <a href={file("api/src/worker/demand.ts")}><code>api/src/worker/demand.ts</code></a> and{" "}
      <a href={file("api/src/worker/index.ts")}><code>api/src/worker/index.ts</code></a>, the beacon in{" "}
      <a href={file("site/src/components/Beacon.astro")}><code>Beacon.astro</code></a> and{" "}
      <a href={file("site/src/components/Search.astro")}><code>Search.astro</code></a>, and the nightly rollup in{" "}
      <a href={file("workers/rollup/")}><code>workers/rollup/</code></a>. All of it is public.
    </p>
  </section>
</Docs>

<style>
  .doc :global(#code code) {
    font-size: 0.88em;
  }
</style>
